<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Best</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1899–1978</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Charles Herbert Best</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Best was an American-born Canadian medical scientist who, as a student assistant to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-banting">Frederick Banting</a> at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a>, helped isolate insulin in 1921–1922. The discovery transformed the treatment of diabetes. He later succeeded J. J. R. Macleod as professor of physiology at Toronto and led research on heparin and other subjects.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-best.jpg" alt="Charles Best" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gar Lunney · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dr._Charles_H._Best_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
